A||N||7||Puppy_cat||Reliable  to Fiero Owners||||5||
Z||000000||Puppy_cat||10-01-2000||03:34 PM||||Are Fiero's really reliable or are they peices of junk that need lots of upgrades so they wont break down?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||10-01-2000||03:40 PM||||They're probably as reliable as any 13-17 year old car. Any car this age that's still on the road has probably had many problem components replaced.<P>And you don't have problems with rusting body panels or dents either.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-01-2000||04:11 PM||||I agree with deceler8..How many 84-88 Fords, Chevys or Chrysler K cars are still on the road? Or even Camaros, Trans Ams or Firebirds.<P>So if you find an old Fiero out there the chances are it will have 150000 miles on it and will need engine rebuild, transmission work, suspension work and some reupolstry.  The same sort of stuff that an 85 Buick would need.<P>There are so low milage ones around, so you pay upfront for low milage.||||reg||4
Z||000003||GodSend||10-01-2000||06:08 PM||||Puppy_Cat,<P>  Being a recent 87'g gt owner myself I have to say that these things are fairly easy to take care of.  I had absolute maintenance nightmares with my 91 Eagle Talon.  You know, 1000$ for a Timing belt change, 1500 for a clutch, and so forth.<P>  I find that parts for these cars are very reasonably priced (with the exception of that dam EGR TUBE).  I find its a nice engine to tinker around with.  I have a small idle hunt problem on mine which I was able to fix , something I also had and could never attempt to fix myself.<P>  Definatly try to find the lowest milage one you can.  I am also from Canada and there are ALOT of people who store their fiero's during the winter, so finding one with nice milage is not that difficult.<P>  I purchased my 87' with 96,000 km's a few month ago (just went past 100,000 this weekend), and she is going away for the winter in about a month.  The car is purchased is really nice.  No faded interior, no cracks, burns, or peeling.  The paint is a1 (has a 14 later paint job) - Look hard, and check out these sites for some Canadian price info <BR> <A HREF="http://www.trader.ca" TARGET=_blank>http://www.trader.ca</A> <P>Have fun<P>Godsend<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Seanh||10-01-2000||06:59 PM||||Fieros are great cars. The only ones that brake down and are peices of junk are ones that were not properly taken care of, and their owners did not know of this forum either. I have an 86 I bought almost 2 years ago with 114K on it. I dont drive alot, just to school, work, and then home. I just reached 118K a couple weeks ago. I am having some trouble with the cooling system now, and it might be a little messy trying to fix it, but it has been the first major problem with the car. Thats somthing you have to expect with a car this old. I am going to completely rebuild my car this winter to remove all rust and get it to like new condition. They are a very reliable car considering the age and money. There also the funnest.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||yobtah||10-01-2000||07:22 PM||||They definitely seem more reliable than other _comparable_ cars from the same years.  Your average Fiero is probably in much better running condition than your average mid-eighties Chevy Celebrity or Pontiac 6000.  I often think a bit of extra TLC went into Fieros compared to other GM products.  That's not saying much, though.  My '87 Fiero hasn't been nearly as reliable as my '89 Acura Integra... and the Integra has almost twice the mileage.||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||10-02-2000||09:34 AM||||  Well, I'd say since I've had mine it has been pretty reliable.  I had a couple DNF's due to a hacked engine harness (Still don't know how a simple 4 cyl engine replacement could go so bad?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )  Once I replaced the harness (plug & play) and a $13 temp gauge sensor it runs great.  <P>I figure if I have $200 worth of work to repair something maybe once a year it's better than paying $200+ for a new car payment every month, plus new cars are not immune to problems themselves.  One of the first things my Fiero needed was an alternator, replaced it for about $50 bucks and had it in the car in about an hour.  Around the same time the alternator in my parents '95 Monte Carlo Z24 went out.  Cost them $300 mostly for labor.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">        <BR>        <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||whamel||10-02-2000||10:01 AM||||All of mine have been very reliable once all the bugs have been worked out.  Main points I have seen that drive up the price a bunch were in the '88 models when repairing brake and suspension parts.  I was told the best price for a 4 wheel brake repair would be about $1400 for my '88 coupe and I paid a significant amount to have the shocks and struts replaced.  Once I did that though, the car ran for a good 100,000 miles before it was stolen.  Only other significant problem was that the brake cable frayed and touched the block somewhere causing the starter to go nuts.  I should have grabbed the camera and got a shot of my Fiero up in a tree.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Wade<BR>||||reg||1
